about two weeks ago on twitter one of  the people who i follow issued a very  interesting challenge which had to do  with documents and with the wombo ai art  generator  and the suggestion was that we should  take our theses and dissertations titles  whether at the phd or masters or  bachelor's level  and input those as prompts to see what  kind of imagery  wombo would come up with so i decided to  do that  with i actually have all three a  bachelor's  honors thesis a master's thesis and a  phd dissertation the phd dissertation  title was a little bit too long to use  as a prompt but my bachelor's worked  just fine and it's  a document that i no longer have i'm  sure it's stored somewhere at lakeland  university and it was called  truth self-referential isomorphic  statement groups  and you might say well what the hell is  that about well so  i was very much into the intersections  between philosophy and mathematics and  logic  at the time  and you know the notion of using  mathematics to represent things was you  know kind of a cool idea i was playing  around with and when you've got  isomorphisms things are are connected  with each other in interesting ways  where there's a mapping function going  on  and truth self-referential means that  you have a statement that either refers  to its own truth or is part of a set of  statements that are all connected  together that in some way refer to each  other's truth so the liars paradox is a  prime example as a matter of fact played  an important role in that the liar's  paradox is very simple you can do it in  in several different ways one is for me  to say i am lying to you right now right  if it's true then i'm lying to you so  then it's false but if it's false then  it means i'm not lying to you so i'm  telling the truth you see where this is  going and so i was playing around with a  lot of these sorts of things and i you  know i don't need to go through what the  thesis was about instead i'm much more  interested in  what this was about so isomorphic truth  suffer self-referential statement groups  right a nice title  what does wombo generate you see some of  the images there behind me and we're  going to look at  all of the images that it generated  in just a bit people get very  preoccupied i'll say is the last thing  about is this art or not who's the  artist and i don't really care about  that sort of stuff frankly myself  that's issues for other people who are  much more concerned about that i'm  really interested in what kind  of cool imagery does this generate so  i'm going to take you on a little trip  through what this  title isomorphic truth self referential  statement groups  provides us with and let's see what it  looks like  here is the first image that was  generated using that prompt from my  bachelor's honors thesis and i like this  one quite a bit it looks almost like a  cloud city  but when we start looking at it from  going from the top to the bottom we see  some cool and interesting features first  of all there's a person in the landscape  at the top and then as we scroll down we  see something like houses and buildings  stacked on top of each other and it goes  all the way down to a base it's not just  hanging there in the clouds  when we turn to the next one again we  see very architectural features to this  but now there's a clear explicit  addition  of human beings one of the artistic  themes that is going to run through  quite a few of these so if we begin at  the top we see  this beautiful you know city sort of  built up out of other bits and pieces  hanging up in the sky and then we scroll  our way down  and we see human-like figures none of  them are particularly great  people constructions and some of them  are a bit grotesque but you can identify  them as people and they're there in the  middle of the city perhaps looking up at  it if we look at them very closely you  can see that you know what we've got  here  does look like it has faces in some  places or hands but it's rather  grotesque and some of them blend in a  bit at points with the city itself  now in the next one we have a very  different kind of motif  one that just has  patterns that are renderland of writing  perhaps or some sort of script  and where they're all bunched up on top  of each other if we  scroll down through this we see that  there's no writing anywhere in it but  there's writing like things and some of  them are nested inside of each other  very colorful very cool i think  this is another one that's quite similar  but it's not quite so colorful it's much  more  muted and if we look at this one we'll  go from the bottom to the top same sort  of deal we're not seeing an actual  script we're seeing something that it  that imitates it or apes it and maybe  could be interpreted as if we zoom in  and we look  at this  non-writing  this interesting calligraphy or  cuneiform or pictograms there's no  actual  information there is there there's only  the hint of it  going on again  we managed to combine both of these  themes in this one which is actually my  favorite of all of the ones generated  for isomorphic truth self-referential  statement groups and if we  zoom in on it once again this luminous  beautiful semi-writing is not quite  writing as such but it's worth checking  out and then we have the people down  here it seems to be that the theme is  putting together a number of robed  people who maybe are conferring about  these matters and we can like scroll  from them up into this writing and it's  almost as if it's  you know a monolith hanging over them  that they're interpreting of course  that's very subjective on my part  here we have something that almost looks  more like a library at first and from  the outset we're looking at it and it  looks like there's humanoid figures  involved with maybe books or  writing behind them and then you know a  basis of a book but if we start at the  top and we work our way downward  maybe this isn't actually human beings  maybe this is just garments or even like  skin suits that have been thrown  together  as we go down further we get something  that could be a human being  seeded we also get something that looks  suspiciously like a chicken  and then we have this bottom part which  almost looks like a book itself turned  on its side  then we get a really trippy one and this  is another one that i particularly like  where we combine the humanoid figure  with the  potential writing  if we start at the top we have this you  know really trippy  kind of background that is is less  linear more circular now and we go down  to these figures who now look more  owl-like than human  perhaps or avian at least all the way  down to  the  things that they are surrounded by or  surrounding  and those are those are kind of cool if  we zoom in on it we can see like here's  a  figure with that  what do we want to call it like wall of  of writing or symbols  so that that's an interesting one here's  another really interesting one um this  one it's more like a composite of a  giant figure with a head whose head is  composed of many other things it's it is  i think the steampunk one and there's a  lot of other stuff going on in this one  if we begin at the bottom we see  something that could be a little ship  perhaps uh some some mountains and it  leads upward into the figure who is  composed of all sorts of deformities and  monstrosities  tied together uh if we look at it more  closely there's faces within it as well  and then it fades off into the  background  here's one as well that also has a large  figure and  something that looks like a face if we  begin at the bottom we have what appear  to be more architectural features and  again massive shoulders leading up into  something that's clearly a  face there's eyes and a nose to it and  then the  helmet or headdress or hair is composed  of all sorts of other stuff as well this  is a really interesting one and i  believe this was the pastel  here we have just a single figure or  what appears to be a figure  because  well there's two things to say so we'll  come back to the figure in a moment look  at the ground the ground is more  circular or ovaloid  but it also does have that same  appearance of like conveying  information or script but now it's the  land itself now if we zoom in on the  figure and we get closer and closer  it looks on the one hand less and less  like a human being  more and more as if it's just part of  the landscape and we get to see that  the landscape itself  does look quite a bit different than  some of the other ones that we've seen  here's the last one this is another  favorite of mine in part because of the  celestial imagery of it it's quite  beautiful  and if we start at the bottom we have  some human figures we have some you know  some  some painterly stuff that i myself would  love to be able to do we go up into  through this this curve and we have a  number of other seemingly humanoid  figures in various stances and then we  have the  luminous  heavens themselves again very brushworky  quite  beautiful so those are the ones that are  generated by  using this prompt that derives from the  title of my senior thesis  isomorphic truth self referential  statement groups and we'll do more of  these in the future with my master's  thesis and my dissertation and see what  comes out of those  you
 

